<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Partial plist: everything else is generated from build settings
	  (GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE merges this file in), the same arrangement the
	  app and the widget use.

	  This exists for the NSExtension keys, which have no INFOPLIST_KEY_* form.
	  Setting one is silently ignored rather than rejected: the build still
	  succeeds, the extension is still embedded, and the app then fails to
	  install with a placeholder error that names neither the extension nor the
	  key.
	-->
	<key>NSExtension</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSExtensionPointIdentifier</key>
		<string>com.apple.share-services</string>
		<key>NSExtensionPrincipalClass</key>
		<string>$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME).ShareViewController</string>
		<key>NSExtensionAttributes</key>
		<dict>
			<!--
			  What makes Haven appear in the share sheet at all.

			  Web URLs, because a shared profile is one; images, so importing a
			  screenshot works before the photo library has ever been asked
			  for; text, because LinkedIn's own app is one of the three
			  this exists for and proved, on device, on 2026-08-04, that it
			  shares a profile as a plain-text message with the link inside
			  it, not as a URL attachment -- the exact case an earlier version
			  of this comment named and set aside rather than guessed at; and
			  vCards, because the Contacts app's own "Share Contact" hands over
			  nothing else. The accepted cost: Haven now appears in the sheet
			  for any text share, LinkedIn profile or not. ShareInput.swift is
			  where that cost is paid down -- a text share that does not
			  contain a recognizable profile link, or a vCard that names nobody
			  worth saving, lands back on the same cannot-read state a broken
			  URL already did, so the sheet appearing is not the same as the
			  sheet ever saving something wrong.

			  A predicate string, not the dictionary form
			  (NSExtensionActivationSupportsWebURLWithMaxCount /
			  ...SupportsImageWithMaxCount) this used to be. The dictionary
			  form requires every attachment in the payload to be claimed by a
			  declared key -- and Safari's own share payload for a webpage
			  bundles companions alongside the URL (page text, a snapshot
			  image reference, a plist) that no key here claims, so the whole
			  payload was rejected and the extension never appeared for a
			  Safari share at all. This SUBQUERY form asks a different
			  question -- does at least one attachment qualify, ignoring
			  whatever else rode along -- which is what the dictionary form
			  cannot express.

			  public.url, public.image and public.plain-text each carry an
			  added "and it is not a local file" -- public.url because
			  public.file-url conforms to it, so a Files-app share of a
			  document would otherwise pass as a web URL; public.image and
			  public.plain-text for the same reason applied to a Files-app
			  share of an image or text file, keeping "images" scoped to what
			  Photos hands over (image data, no file URL) and "text" scoped to
			  what an app's own share sheet composes (a message, no file URL)
			  rather than either to anything sitting on disk. That is the
			  predicate form of "not arbitrary file shares" above.

			  public.vcard carries no such exclusion: a .vcf shared from Files
			  is still, contents and all, an actual vCard rather than a
			  document that merely happens to conform to one of the other
			  three UTIs by accident of format, so there is no mislabeling risk
			  to guard against the way there is for a URL, an image or text.

			  Verified against Apple's real App Extension Programming Guide
			  (archived; the current developer.apple.com page dropped the
			  worked examples) for the SUBQUERY / $extensionItem / $attachment
			  / registeredTypeIdentifiers / UTI-CONFORMS-TO grammar, and by
			  compiling this exact string with NSPredicate(format:) against
			  hand-built Safari-shaped, Files-shaped and LinkedIn-text-shaped
			  payloads before it went in this file -- the guide's own two
			  examples use ".@count == $extensionItem.attachments.@count",
			  which demands every attachment match and would keep the very bug
			  this predicate exists to fix; ">= 1" here is what actually
			  ignores companions.
			-->
			<key>NSExtensionActivationRule</key>
			<string>SUBQUERY (
				extensionItems,
				$extensionItem,
				SUBQUERY (
					$extensionItem.attachments,
					$attachment,
					(ANY $attachment.registeredTypeIdentifiers UTI-CONFORMS-TO "public.url" AND NOT (ANY $attachment.registeredTypeIdentifiers UTI-CONFORMS-TO "public.file-url"))
					OR
					(ANY $attachment.registeredTypeIdentifiers UTI-CONFORMS-TO "public.image" AND NOT (ANY $attachment.registeredTypeIdentifiers UTI-CONFORMS-TO "public.file-url"))
					OR
					(ANY $attachment.registeredTypeIdentifiers UTI-CONFORMS-TO "public.plain-text" AND NOT (ANY $attachment.registeredTypeIdentifiers UTI-CONFORMS-TO "public.file-url"))
					OR
					(ANY $attachment.registeredTypeIdentifiers UTI-CONFORMS-TO "public.vcard")
				).@count >= 1
			).@count >= 1</string>
		</dict>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
